****
Of the Fraunhofer-lines, the G-band is perhaps the only "wide line" (at around 2nm, around 430nm) that is 
a dense barcode of several subtle lines, each from their own chemical, instead of being one single element, like H alpha. 
It is far enough away from the otherwise obvious but not outstanding H gamma (at around 434nm), to not be confused/contaminated by it.

The G-band is dominated by iron and calcium, with several lines of the molecule CH present in the Sun's atmosphere. 
The general aspect of the Sun at this wavelength (observed through a 2nm FWHM etalon, centered to the G-band) 
is close to the wing of the Calcium K and H lines. A generic blue-Sun image, neutral-metal-Sun, with higher-contrast plages and darker spots.

Recent studies indicate that one can see prominences and flares in this spectral region, 
tied primarily to the subtle lines of ionized titanium and neutral calcium present around here, while ruling out the contribution of the molecular lines. 
Nothing spectacular, when it comes to the entirity of the G-band. In theory, there are enough lines here, and the continuum is attenuated enough, 
that brighter prominences should show up in an etalon image, especially if a coronagraph or other tricky instruments are deployed. 
Way more tricky than just a simple full disk observing etalon, like with H alpha, but perhaps easier than trying a continuum-surrounded wavelength.

Thus, while the flares and prominences do show up in the spectral region historically called G-band, 
instead of "G-band prominence" it is perhaps better to go with the lines in question, like Ca I 4308 or Sc II 4306.
